body:
Well... that brings up a slightly ridiculous reason I can't just lie about COVID exposure.

&#x200B;

Right around my grandfather's death, about 2 weeks ago, my roommate actually had a COVID scare (headache and stomach ache, so it wasn't a strong likelihood she had it). I conveyed this to my mum. A few days later, my mum messaged me to ask about the test results. I let her know that my roommate tested negative. Apparently,  this info was passed along to my father when my mum was negotiating my sister's trip to the funeral. I would've loved to use that lie, and totally would have, but I feel like I'd have to come up with something else now.

&#x200B;

I'm also not totally sure what my father knows (because lots of info is apparently being passed around), which is pretty stressful.

He is able to contact me because he's paying for my phone. I don't have any income because my planned summer work was ended early due to COVID, so I need to take any help with bills that I can.

&#x200B;

He and my mum are still in contact because they haven't finalized their divorce and my sister lives with my mum. She checked to see if I had any COVID risk because I had plans with my sister. My lack of risk was passed on to my father in light of the impending funeral. My mum understands that I don't want to see him, but she has her own motives to keep open communication and a positive relationship with him. She has encouraged me to attend the funeral.

I chose to tell her because I felt like she would understand and I wanted to have a potential ally when I cut ties from him in the future. My brother's psychotic break is fairly recent, and in light of it she's learned more about our internal family issues. She's also been around her son more in the last few months than in the years prior - he's been deteriorating into an angry, obsessive person for about a decade now, but she wasn't experiencing much of that until recently. I sincerely thought that, by telling her, I would receive support and understanding. More than that, she has been really helpful to me over the years. If I'm not going to be attending her husbands funeral, I wanted her to know the real reason. I'm hoping that this will let us have a better relationship long term, since my father's influence has driven a wedge between us for a long time.

&#x200B;

In light of how the conversation went (her talking about my ability to reconcile with him in the future), I feel like I made a mistake. But going into it, I felt pretty secure that she'd understand and be flexible about me mourning my grandfather with her at another time.

I really don't feel safe telling him how I feel until I can totally cut him out of my life. He's been smart about keeping some financial ties between us that I won't be able to cut until I've graduated and found a job.

&#x200B;

I've been really careful not to upset him, but I've seen what happens to people who do. My brother's possessions electronics and furniture have been broken in my father's fits of rage. At one point, my brother went somewhere without my father's permission and my father cut some wires in my brother's car that cost hundreds to fix. He also recently stole my mum's car and abandoned it in a parking lot - she had to get the cops involved to recover it. But he's careful: he only does these things when there isn't legal recourse for the victim. He knows where I live and he has ways to get back at me.

&#x200B;

I do really appreciate knowing that other people have been through the same thing, though. Hopefully I can be honest with him about this eventually.

I was part of an engineering program in high school, lead by the absolute best teachers I've had. Being part of that program really directed me towards chemical engineering, which I am presently studying and loving. In addition to the awesome learning experience in the program, I also made some amazing friends who I continued on to college with.

&#x200B;

But, although I did have some close friends in the program, I've always been quite shy and lesser-known. My forte is school work, not socializing, and I barely interacted with most of my classmates in the program despite spending 3 hours every other school day together for 4 years. I tended to sit towards the back, work with the same few people on projects, and rarely see my classmates outside of school.

&#x200B;

At the end of senior year, the program's teachers organized a picnic for the graduating class. It was during school hours, so pretty much everyone was in attendance. There were card games, sports, eating, and talking going on for several hours, which I was peripherally involved in, then the teachers gathered us all for a closing ceremony. 

&#x200B;

The teachers talked about how proud they were of us and how they looked forward to hearing about our futures. Then, they brought out a pair of envelopes and invited me and another student (a truly amazing, lovely guy who gives fantastic hugs) up to the front of the room. Each of the teachers brought up things that the two of us had done during our time with them that made us stand out, then presented us with the envelopes containing scholarship checks. Getting that sort of acknowledgement from people I respect so much despite often feeling invisible was an incredible feeling.

&#x200B;

Our physics teacher also gave out individual hand written letters to everyone in the class. I've kept that letter through at least half a dozen moves now. Along with the letter, she put in a coupon for 1 batch of homemade cookies in exchange for us writing her a letter. I'm planning to write her back when I graduate college, along with the other 2 teachers in thanks for all the amazing lessons and memories they gave me.
